00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:03.120 align:start position:0%
the clock is ticking to save the world  from  catastrophic climate change but in a key  announcement  britain says it will speed up its  reduction of dangerous greenhouse gases  in the next  decade and hopes other countries will  follow suit  under the paris agreement almost all  countries say they'll put forward new  targets for cutting emissions called  nationally determined contributions  the uk has currently reduced its  emissions by 45  since 1990. in a previous pledge it said  it would cut them by 53  by 2030 now the uk has declared its  new ndc will cut 68 percent of emissions  within a decade the legally binding  target is to reduce  emissions overall by a hundred percent  by 2050  to reach net zero britain  is uh out ahead of most of the other big  nations it it's committed more than the  eu is likely to do  it's more than the u.s is likely to be  able to do it certainly more than the  likes of australia  uh have done but britain's promises  can't come quick enough for countries  like bangladesh  this year once again battered by  cyclones and floods  speaking from the capital dhaka  scientist sally mulhock  told me britain and others need to do  more  we are at the end of receiving those  climate impacts so they may  like to pat themselves on the back and  we have to acknowledge they've done a  bit  it's not not that they aren't doing  anything but they're  simply not doing enough and the enough  is what matters it is whether they can  affect the impacts on the climate  of the climate on the most vulnerable  countries and communities  and they're not going to be able to do  that the way we live our lives is  changing so we can reduce the amount of  greenhouse gases we produce  it's hoped the uk's promises will  trigger a new round of global  commitments  to turn the tide on climate change  before it's too late  lisa holland sky news
 

